US teachers earn 20% less than other graduates with equivalent education and experience — the 'teacher penalty' is the largest in the OECD and growing.
South Korea pays teachers in the top quarter of national salary distribution; Finland's teacher training is as selective as medicine (10% acceptance rate); both countries perform at the top of PISA rankings.
The US loses 44% of new teachers within 5 years — attrition rates are highest in high-poverty schools, creating a 'teacher desert' that compounds disadvantage for the students who most need stability.
